¥≈©¯=˚i]"ô{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A record type describes the fields a value supplies. GCSL uses structural\ntypes: two declarations with the same structure describe the same type,\nand a record can be used where fewer of its fields are required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"declaring-a-shape\">Declaring a shape <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#declaring-a-shape\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Use <code>type</code> to give a structure a reusable name. An annotation checks a\nvalue against that structure. Defaults supply omitted fields in a record\nliteral checked against the type:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Install = {from: String, exclude: List&lt;String&gt; = []}\nlet item: Install = {from = \"src\"}\nitem.exclude\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Defaults are constant expressions, not arbitrary function calls. They are\napplied where a record literal has the expected type; they don't add\nfields to an already constructed variable with a smaller type.</p>\n<p>An annotation also determines what fields later code can see. Extra\nfields in a record literal produce a warning when an expected type hides\nthem. A structural alias does not introduce a distinct runtime identity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"generic-types\">Generic types <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#generic-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A type can take type parameters, named in angle brackets after its name.\nEach use names the types to put in their place:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Pair&lt;A, B&gt; = {first: A, second: B}\nlet entry: Pair&lt;String, Int&gt; = {first = \"port\"; second = 8080}\nentry.second\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A use must give as many type arguments as the type has parameters, and a\ntype without parameters takes none. Because types are structural,\n<code>Pair&lt;String, Int&gt;</code> is the same type as <code>{first: String, second: Int}</code>. A\ngeneric type can be built from another, as in\n<code>type Named&lt;T&gt; = Pair&lt;String, T&gt;</code>. It can't refer to itself, and it can't\nspread one of its parameters, because the parameter might not be a record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"enums\">Enums <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#enums\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An extensible enum is a type whose variants any module can add. Declare\nthe enum, with an optional bound that every variant's payload must fit,\nand then its variants, each with a payload type:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Shape extensible&lt;{*: Int}&gt;\nenum Shape::Square&lt;{side: Int}&gt;\nenum Shape::Point\nsquare = Shape::Square({side = 3})\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A variant is also a value: <code>Shape::Square</code> on its own has the type\n<code>Variant&lt;Shape, {side: Int}&gt;</code>. Calling it makes a value of the enum,\nand you can pass it to a function as a key. <code>kind.matches(value)</code> tests\nwhether a value is of that variant, and <code>kind.payload(value)</code> takes out\nits payload:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Tool extensible\nenum Tool::C&lt;{cc: String}&gt;\nfn find&lt;T&gt;(kind: Variant&lt;Tool, T&gt;, tools: List&lt;Tool&gt;, fallback: T): T {\n    for t in tools { if kind.matches(t) { return kind.payload(t) } }\n    fallback\n}\ncc = find(Tool::C, [Tool::C({cc = \"gcc\"})], {cc = \"cc\"}).cc\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Unlike records, enums are nominal: two enums are different types even\nif they look alike. <code>value is Tool</code> tests whether a value is of the enum.\nValues of an enum are compared with <code>==</code> only when the enum has a bound,\nbecause an unbounded payload might hold a function. A variant is visible\nin its own file, to the other files of its directory and the directories\nbelow it, and to every file that imports its module.</p>\n<h2 id=\"partial-records\">Partial records <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#partial-records\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>Partial&lt;T&gt;</code> holds any of the fields of the record type <code>T</code>. Use it to\ndescribe a record by only some of its fields, and <code>.matches()</code> to test\na whole record against it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}\nlet wanted: Partial&lt;Platform&gt; = {arch = \"x86_64\"}\nfits = wanted.matches({arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"})\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A field of a partial record can't be read by name, because it may be\nabsent.</p>\n<h2 id=\"records-of-any-fields\">Records of any fields <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#records-of-any-fields\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A rest-field, <code>*: T</code>, gives the type of every field that a record type\ndoesn't name. Use it when the field names are data, such as paths. A\n<code>{*: String}</code> holds any fields, each a <code>String</code>. Named fields keep their\nown types, so <code>{to: String, *: Int}</code> holds a <code>String</code> field <code>to</code>, and any\nother fields are <code>Int</code>s:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let files: {*: String} = {\"bin/*\" = \"bin/*\"; \"sbin/*\" = \"sbin/*\"}\nfiles\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Write the record literal where the type is expected: in an annotation,\nan argument or a definition's result. There, each field that the type\ndoesn't name is checked against the rest-field. A value whose own type\nhas no rest-field doesn't fit one, because a record keeps fields that its\nstatic type hides, and they could be of any type.</p>\n<p>Read a named field as usual. A field that only the rest-field covers\ncan't be read by name, and a record whose type has a rest-field can't be\nspread. Typed equality compares every field, and <code>is</code> and <code>as</code> test each\nfield that the rest-field covers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"combining-fields\">Combining fields <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#combining-fields\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Dotted definitions construct nested records. Compatible dotted paths\nmerge, including across files in a workspace:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>server.host = \"localhost\"\nserver.port = 8080\nserver.tls.enabled = true\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A whole-field definition such as <code>server = {...}</code> closes that field to\nfurther dotted extensions. Define the individual dotted leaves when you\nwant several files to contribute to a shared structure.</p>\n<p>A spread starts from another record's visible fields. Inside an ordinary\nrecord block, put defaults first and explicit overrides afterward:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let defaults = {host = \"localhost\"; port = 80}\nlet config = {\n    ...defaults\n    port = 8080\n}\nconfig.port\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A spread copies only fields visible through its operand's static type.\nIt cannot replace an earlier explicit field inside an ordinary block.\nTop-level declarations are resolved across the whole input and take\nprecedence over a spread of the same name regardless of textual order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unions-and-narrowing\">Unions and narrowing <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#unions-and-narrowing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A union, such as <code>Int | String</code>, allows more than one shape. Use <code>is</code> to\ntest a value and <code>as</code> for a checked conversion that fails at runtime if\nthe value doesn't fit. In <code>if name is Type</code>, the branches see narrowed\ncopies of the named value. This does not perform general flow analysis of\nlater statements or dotted field paths.</p>\n<p>Typed equality <code>==</code> compares operands of the same static type. Total\nequality <code>===</code> compares complete runtime records, including fields hidden by\ntheir shared static type. Both require the same static operand type. The\nlanguage's\nrestrictions on unions, functions, and futures are described in\n<code>gcsl(5)</code> and <a href=\"/gcsl/language/inference-limits\">Inference Limits</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"declaring-a-shape","title":"Declaring a shape","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"generic-types","title":"Generic types","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"enums","title":"Enums","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"partial-records","title":"Partial records","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"records-of-any-fields","title":"Records of any fields","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"combining-fields","title":"Combining fields","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"unions-and-narrowing","title":"Unions and narrowing","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[["gcsl/inference-limits",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/inference-limits","Inference Limits"]}]],"anchors":[]},"refs":[]}